.TH VMSTAT 1M
.SH NAME
vmstat \- report system activity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vmstat
[
.B \-p
]
[ interval [ count ] ]
.br
.B vmstat \-s
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vmstat
delves into the system and reports statistics about processes, memory,
the two block devices, the kernel's four rate counters and the
distribution of processor time.
It reads everything it prints through
.IR kctl (2),
which is unprivileged, and opens no device.
.PP
The optional
.I interval
argument causes
.I vmstat
to report once each
.I interval
seconds.
The first report is for all time since a reboot and each
subsequent report is for the last interval only.
The optional
.I count
argument restricts the number of reports.
With an
.I interval
and no
.IR count ,
.I vmstat
reports until it is stopped.
With neither, it prints one report and exits.
.PP
The columns, in the order printed:
.TP
.B "procs"
.B r
processes runnable and in core;
.B b
processes asleep at or below
.BR PZERO ,
which is to say blocked on a resource rather than waiting for something
interruptible;
.B w
processes runnable but swapped out.
The swapper is not counted.
.TP
.B "memory"
.B avm
the swappable images of every live process, in words;
.B fre
the free extents of
.BR coremap ,
also in words;
and, with
.BR \-p ,
.B txt
the percentage of
.B avm
that is shared text in core.
.TP
.B "swap"
With
.B \-p
only:
.B i
and
.B o
are images read from and written to the paging store per second.
.TP
.B "disks"
.B md
and
.B mb
are exchanges completed per second by the disk controller and by the
drum.
Each is a controller and not a drive:
.I md
covers all the units of
.BR kernel/dev/md.c .
.IR Iostat (1M)
prints three columns per device out of the same histogram, and neither
report is the other's summary.
.TP
.B "faults"
.B in
device interrupts,
.B sy
system calls and
.B cs
context switches, all per second; with
.BR \-p ,
.B tr
traps as well.
.TP
.B "cpu"
The percentage of processor time spent in user mode, in system mode and
idle; with
.BR \-p ,
`nice' user mode is separated out as
.B ni
instead of being folded into
.BR us .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p
Print the wider report: the four extra columns described above.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the counters as they stand, one per line and named, and exit.
No interval, no process scan and no percentages.
.SH FILES
None.
.I Vmstat
reads
.BR proc ,
.BR text ,
.BR coremap ,
.BR dk_time ,
.BR dk_numb ,
.B dk_wds
and the twelve rate counters through
.IR kctl (2).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
kctl(2), iostat(1M), pstat(1M), ps(1)
.SH BUGS
.PP
.B Note:
.B the interval is measured in ticks accounted, not in wall clock.
There is no
.B boottime
variable on this system and none is wanted:
.B kernel/clock.c
tallies every timer interrupt it services into
.BR dk_time ,
so the sum of that array over
.B HZ
is the uptime, and using it puts every number in a report over the same
denominator \- the rates and the percentages cannot then disagree.
The interval timer is a flip-flop and free-runs, so a tick that arrives
while delivery is blocked is coalesced; what is measured is therefore a
lower bound on elapsed time.
.IR Iostat (1M)
prints under the same convention.
.PP
.B Note:
.B the interrupt column counts the clock.
.B HZ
is 250 here, so
.B in
on an idle machine is about 250 and device traffic is what moves it
above that.
That is what 4BSD's
.B v_intr
counted too.
.PP
.B Note:
.B the four percentages are rounded independently
and may sum to 99 or 101.
There is no floating point on this machine and none is wanted for a
whole percent.
.PP
.B Note:
.BR \-t ,
.B \-i
and
.B \-z
are gone.
The first two printed `not applicable' and exited in the source this was
ported from, and are no more applicable here.
.B \-z
zeroed the counters by writing
.BR /dev/kmem ;
.IR kctl (2)
reserves
.B KCTL_SET
and refuses it, and the differential report is what
.B \-z
was mostly wanted for.
.B \-f
is gone with them: there is no fork accounting on this system.
.PP
.B Note:
.B \-s
reports no name-lookup statistics.
There is no name cache in this kernel, and a counter nobody keeps is
worse than a line nobody prints.
.PP
Things can change while
.I vmstat
is running; the picture it gives is eerily accurate for a moment that
has already passed.
